429 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 224 0 R
/E (The incoming electrical supply to the Guwahati Refinery is generally fed at 132 kV, which is stepped down to 6.3 kV and further to 415 V / 230 V levels depending on the type of load and application. This transformation and distribution are carried out by power transformers and distribution transformers strategically located within the substation premises. The installed transformer capacities vary based on critical load requirements; typically, 132/6.3 kV power transformers are rated around 5 MVA to 10 MVA, while 6.3/0.415 kV distribution transformers can range from 250 kVA to 1 MVA. The substation also includes HT panels \(High Tension\), LT panels \(Low Tension\), VCB \(Vacuum Circuit Breakers\) for isolation and protection, Current Transformers \(CTs\), Potential Transformers \(PTs\), and protective relays to ensure safe and automatic shutdown in case of faults.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
430 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 224 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
431 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 224 0 R
/E ( 1. PCC \(Power Control Center\): These panels handle the main power distribution of the plant at the LT level \(415 V\), supplying power to various MCCs, lighting panels, and utility loads. PCCs are usually equipped with Air Circuit Breakers \(ACBs\), energy meters, and protective relays.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
432 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 224 0 R
/E ( 2. MCC \(Motor Control Center\): MCCs control the operation of electric motors in the refinery, such as pumps, compressors, blowers, etc. These panels include DOL \(Direct-On-Line\), Star-Delta, and VFD \(Variable Frequency Drive\) starters along with protection devices like overload relays, fuses, and isolators.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
433 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 224 0 R
/T (IOCL GUWAHATI REFINERY, NOONMATI, ASSAM, INDIA)
/E (IOCL GUWAHATI REFINERY, NOONMATI, ASSAM, INDIA)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
434 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 224 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
435 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 224 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
436 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 225 0 R
/E (3. PMCC \(Power & Motor Control Center\): A combination of PCC and MCC in a single unit, PMCC panels serve both motor and non-motor loads. These are often used in large areas with mixed load types and help optimize space and control logic.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
437 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 225 0 R
/E ( 4. ASB \(Annunciation & Synchronization Board\): Used for generator and load management. These panels display alarms and fault conditions and help in synchronizing multiple power sources \(such as mains and DG sets\).)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
438 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 225 0 R
/E ( 5. DCDB \(Direct Current Distribution Board\): DCDB panels distribute DC power \(usually 24V or 110V DC\) for control circuits, relays, and instrumentation. These are essential for critical systems that require uninterrupted DC supply.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 225 0 R
/E ( 6. UPS \(Uninterruptible Power Supply\) Panels: UPS panels provide backup AC power to critical loads such as DCS/SCADA systems, emergency lighting, PLC panels, fire and gas systems, etc., in case of main power failure. The UPS system generally includes battery banks, inverters, and static switches.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 225 0 R
/E ( 7. LCP \(Local Control Panels\): Installed near field equipment, LCPs allow local manual control and feedback from motors or instruments. They serve as an interface between the control room and the field.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
441 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 225 0 R
/E ( 8. Relay & Protection Panels: These are used to protect the entire substation and downstream equipment from overcurrent, earth faults, under-voltage, and other faults. They typically house numeric relays, trip circuits, CT/VT input modules, and event loggers.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
442 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 225 0 R
/E ( 9. Lighting Distribution Panels: These panels supply power to industrial and office lighting across refinery zones and are typically isolated from critical process panels to avoid interference.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
443 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 225 0 R
/E (The entire electrical system is monitored from the Main Control Room \(MCR\) and Electrical Control Room, where SCADA \(Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition\) and DCS \(Distributed Control System\) platforms help in real-time monitoring, fault detection, load management, and automation. The substation also includes earthing systems, cable trenches, busbars, and fire protection systems in accordance with safety standards like IEC and IS codes. Fire safety in electrical installations is further supported by flameproof panels and regular IR thermography inspections.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
