/T (A Vacuum Circuit Breaker \(VCB\) is a type of circuit breaker used mainly in medium-voltage systems \(typically 3.3 kV to 33 kV\). In a VCB, arc quenching takes place in a vacuum, which has very high dielectric strength.)
/E (A Vacuum Circuit Breaker \(VCB\) is a type of circuit breaker used mainly in medium-voltage systems \(typically 3.3 kV to 33 kV\). In a VCB, arc quenching takes place in a vacuum, which has very high dielectric strength.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1046 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 258 0 R
/T (When a fault occurs, the breaker contacts separate inside a vacuum interrupter. An arc is formed momentarily, but since there is no ionized medium in vacuum, the arc is extinguished very quickly, usually at the first current zero. This results in fast and reliable interruption of the fault current.)
/E (When a fault occurs, the breaker contacts separate inside a vacuum interrupter. An arc is formed momentarily, but since there is no ionized medium in vacuum, the arc is extinguished very quickly, usually at the first current zero. This results in fast and reliable interruption of the fault current.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
